  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: feb736c698faf364bb8a1eef1ded0910, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 125242945536
    m_Localized: "Protein synthesis begins within the nucleus, when a segment of
      the DNA molecule (gene) corresponding to the protein to be formed is transcribed.
      This process, called transcription, involves copying a gene to form a molecule
      called messenger RNA (mRNA). DNA and RNA are molecules composed of nucleotides.
      Nucleotides consist of a sugar\u2014ribose in RNA, hence called ribonucleotides,
      and deoxyribose in DNA, hence called deoxyribonucleotides\u2014a phosphate,
      and a nitrogenous base. The following nitrogenous bases compose ribonucleotides:
      adenine (A), uracil (U), found exclusively in RNA molecules, cytosine (C),
      and guanine (G), while deoxyribonucleotides are formed by adenine (A), thymine
      (T), found exclusively in DNA molecules, cytosine (C), and guanine (G).RNA
      is formed from a single strand, using a DNA template strand. During this process,
      ribonucleotides pair with DNA nucleotides. Ribonucleotides pair with deoxyribonucleotides
      according to Chargaff's rule: A-T, U-A, C-G, and G-C. There is a specific nucleotide
      sequence in DNA where the transcription process begins: TAC; thus, at the beginning
      of mRNA, there will be a sequence of AUG, called the start codon. The enzyme
      RNA polymerase will link ribonucleotides together, forming mRNA, which is a
      single strand. mRNA is then detached from the DNA gene sequence when it encounters
      sequences on the DNA called \"STOP\": ATT, ATC, ACT, or in other words, transcription
      termination codons. A complex of proteins present in the nucleus will transport
      mRNA through nuclear membrane pores to ribosomes in the cytoplasm."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1
    m_Localized: 'After mRNA molecules bind to ribosomes, another stage of protein
      synthesis begins: the process of translation, where protein molecules are formed.
      Ribosomes consist of two subunits, a larger one and a smaller one, where mRNA
      will bind to initiate translation, i.e., the process of protein synthesis.
      Each sequence of three nucleotides (called codons) on mRNA is translated, with
      the help of another RNA molecule called transfer RNA (tRNA), where each amino
      acid has its tRNA (tRNA-AA). For example, an mRNA of 18 nucleotides with the
      following base sequence: AUG-CAG-UGG-UCA-GCA-UAA corresponds to the amino acids
      methionine, arginine, tryptophan, serine, alanine. The initial codon AUG is
      the initiator (always encoding methionine), while the codons UAA, UAG, UGA
      are the terminators, meaning the ribosome will read the entire mRNA molecule,
      starting at the specific sequence (AUG), until the last, which is the termination
      sequence (STOP). With each added amino acid, they bond together forming a peptide
      bond (linking one amino acid to another), thus a protein is also called a polypeptide
      chain. When it reaches the STOP codon, the polypeptide chain disconnects from
      the ribosome, and translation is complete.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2
    m_Localized: In this next stage, protein processing may occur, meaning they may
      still undergo some modifications that will make them functional. This could
      involve attaching other types of molecules to them, such as carbohydrates,
      forming glycoproteins; lipids, forming lipoproteins; or even metals, such as
      iron, copper, forming metalloproteins. They can also undergo structural modifications
      in the chain to become active. The formed polypeptide chain is called the primary
      structure, which will then be rearranged into three-dimensional structures
      referring to their spatial folding, necessary for exerting their biological
      activities. Secondary structures are represented by alpha helices, beta sheets,
      and loops, and the tertiary structure is the spatial arrangement that secondary
      structures acquire when they "fold" together. In this form, they are active
      and perform their biological functions, referred to as monomers. They can also
      be composed of two or more monomers, forming dimers, trimers, tetramers, and
      other polymeric forms, which are called quaternary structures. These processes
      are carried out by different enzymes and/or protein complexes present in the
      cytoplasm and are essential for the physiological function that the protein
      will perform.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3
    m_Localized: Each protein is produced to perform a specific function, and for
      this to occur, it needs to be transported to its target location. This location
      can be within the cell itself, in other structures such as the nucleus, mitochondria,
      lysosomes, or outside the cells, where they will perform their activities in
      other locations of the organism, being carried by the bloodstream and lymphatic
      system. Many proteins are also stored in the form of pre-proteins in granules,
      which serve as "depots" for these proteins. When the body requires the action
      of that protein, it will be converted into an active protein and released from
      these storage sites, exerting its biological action.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4
    m_Localized: Transcription
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5
